Hashing in Linux

Open the terminal at that path where file is stored:

1. Display SHA-512 hash (no output file:

sha512sum <filename.type>
eg:
sha512sum onlyoffice-desktopeditors_amd64.deb

2. Save only the hash string to a text file :

sha512sum <filename.type> | cut -d' ' -f1 > filename.sha512>
eg:
sha512sum onlyoffice-desktopeditors_amd64.deb | cut -d' ' -f1 > onlyoffice-desktopeditors_amd64.sha512


To check for 128 characters:
hash=$(sha512sum onlyoffice-desktopeditors_amd64.deb | cut -d' ' -f1); echo "$hash"; echo "Length: ${#hash}"

3. To Create hash file :

sha512sum photo.png>photo.png.sha512

4. To check: keep the file (e.g, photo.png)and hash file (photo.png.sha512) in the same folder, open terminal there:

sha512sum -c photo.png.sha512

Output : photo.png: OK (means the file is original)
